  <h2 id="biomaths">Bio-Maths: the fourth-subject combination that changes your options</h2>

  <p>
    Bio-Maths is not a separate stream from PCB or BiPC.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is PCB plus Mathematics carried as a fourth subject, most common in Andhra Pradesh and Telangana junior colleges, and it genuinely widens what comes next.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Plain PCB (Biology, Physics, Chemistry only)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Keeps NEET-track medical routes fully open.</li>
        <li>Most BSc, nursing, and paramedical degrees are unaffected either way.</li>
        <li>Locks out JEE and most core-engineering B.Tech branches, since Maths is usually mandatory there.</li>
        <li>Some private universities still admit PCB into B.Tech Biotechnology with a bridge maths course.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">Bio-Maths (PCB plus Mathematics)</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Keeps NEET-track medical routes open, exactly like plain PCB.</li>
        <li>Adds JEE and EAMCET-linked engineering routes: Biotechnology, Biomedical Engineering, Food Technology, Pharmaceutical Engineering.</li>
        <li>Removes the need for a maths bridge course at universities that require it for biotech-linked B.Tech programs.</li>
        <li>Does not change anything about BSc, nursing, agriculture, or paramedical eligibility — those stay open either way.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    If you are still choosing a stream and Bio-Maths is available at your junior college, it is close to a strictly wider option than plain PCB — the extra subject load is the real trade-off, not any closed door.
  </p>

  <h2 id="btech">B.Tech courses for PCB and Bio-Maths students</h2>

  <p>
    "Can PCB students do B.Tech" is one of the most searched, and most poorly answered, questions in this whole space.
  </p>

  <p>
