  </p>

  <h2 id="eamcet">EAMCET, CUET, and what actually changes by entrance exam</h2>

  <p>
    EAMCET (both AP EAMCET and TS EAMCET) is a state-specific entrance exam, mainly relevant if you are applying for engineering, pharmacy, or agriculture seats within Andhra Pradesh or Telangana.
  </p>

  <p>
    For BiPC students there, it admits directly into B.Tech Biotechnology, Biomedical Engineering, Pharmaceutical Engineering, Food Technology, B.Pharm, Pharm.D, BSc Agriculture, Horticulture, Forestry, Veterinary Science, and Fisheries Science — no NEET required for any of these.
  </p>

  <p>
    Outside those two states, most BSc life-science, nursing, and agriculture programs admit through CUET UG or direct merit on 12th marks, and nursing specifically usually runs its own separate entrance test regardless of state.
  </p>

  <p class="note-line">
    Exam-specific eligibility rules change often and vary by university. <a href="/blog/career-options/career-in-biotechnology-after-12th/">Career in biotechnology after 12th</a> and <a href="/blog/stream-selection/cuet-vs-jee-which-is-better-for-career/">CUET vs JEE, which is better for your career</a> go deeper into exam-by-exam comparison. Verify current rules on the <a href="https://cuet.nta.nic.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">official CUET UG portal</a> and your state's official EAMCET website before finalising a plan.
  </p>

